\title{A Corpus on zkVMs and SNARKs}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    [zkVMs and SNARKs](dhsorens-002C) is a new corpus on this site: a systematic study of the cryptography and machine architecture these systems are built from, one page per topic. It follows the pipeline every modern proof system follows, from a computation to a deployed proof, and it is the theory behind the [snarkification work](dhsorens-0020).
}

\p{
    The sections:
    \ul{
        \li{[The shape of a proof system](dhsorens-002D) — the definitions everything else is stated in, and where security is lost between an interactive protocol and a deployed one.}
        \li{[The algebraic and coding-theoretic toolkit](dhsorens-002J) — fields, polynomials, Reed–Solomon codes, proximity gaps, sumcheck.}
        \li{[Arithmetisation](dhsorens-002R) — R1CS, Plonkish, AIR, lookups, and [Clean](dhsorens-002Y).}
        \li{[Polynomial commitment schemes](dhsorens-002Z), and [low-degree testing](dhsorens-0036) from FRI through STIR to WHIR.}
        \li{[Proof systems in the wild](dhsorens-003D) and [folding, recursion, and aggregation](dhsorens-003K).}
        \li{[zkVMs](dhsorens-003Q) — the stack, the systems in the field, and how they break.}
        \li{[Formalising the stack](dhsorens-003Z) — [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040), [CompPoly](dhsorens-0041), and the rest of the verified-zkEVM effort.}
        \li{[The Ethereum setting](dhsorens-0046), and [a reading path](dhsorens-004B) through all of it.}
    }
}
